>> title: Harvest Chopped Salad
>> category: Salads
>> tags: kale, squash, pecans, apple, goat cheese
>> counters: The Bowl Shop
>> aka: fall harvest chopped, harvest salad, autumn chopped salad, fall salad, squash and kale salad, chopped kale salad
>> pairs-with: basic-vinaigrette
>> servings: 4
>> time: 50 min
>> slack: forgiving — every component holds a day in the fridge separately, and the salad is twenty seconds of tossing at the end
>> step.2: roast 425°F 25 min
>> step.3: toast 6 min, cool hard
>> step.4: rub 1 min
>> step.5: chop in
>> step.6: toss

Preheat the #oven{} to 425°F (220°C).

Roast @delicata squash{1%lb}(450 g; halved, seeded, sliced in 1/2-in half-moons) with @extra-virgin olive oil{2%Tbs}(30 mL) and @kosher salt{1/2%tsp} on a #sheet pan{} for ~roast{25%min}, cut side down and not moved for the first fifteen. Delicata is the one winter squash nobody has to peel — the skin goes tender in the oven and holds the half-moon together, which is exactly what a chopped salad needs and what a peeled cube of butternut cannot do.

Toast @pecans{1%cup}(110 g) with @maple syrup{2%Tbs}(30 mL), @kosher salt{1/4%tsp} and @cayenne{1/8%tsp} in a #skillet{} over medium heat for ~toast{6%min}, then spread them on parchment to ~cool{10%min} in a single layer. They are sticky and soft in the pan and only go hard as they cool, so a pile left to cool in a heap sets as one pecan brick.

Rub @lacinato kale{1%bunch}(stemmed, chopped in 1/2-in pieces) with @kosher salt{1/4%tsp} and @apple cider vinegar{1%Tbs}(15 mL) for ~rub{1%min}. A minute, not the two a kale caesar wants: here the kale is one of five things in the bowl rather than the whole salad, and rubbed all the way down it disappears under the squash.

Chop @honeycrisp apple{1%large}(cored, in 1/2-in dice), @celery{2%ribs}(sliced thin) and @dried cranberries{1/3%cup} into @&(~1)kale{}.

Toss @&(~1)chopped salad{} with @&(~4)roasted squash{}, @&(~3)candied pecans{}, @goat cheese{3%oz}(85 g; crumbled) and @basic vinaigrette{1/3%cup}(80 mL) while the squash is still warm.

Warm squash against cold vinaigrette is the whole feel of this salad and the reason a shop keeps the squash in a hotel pan rather than the fridge. Dress it cold and it is the same ingredients and a duller plate.
